    slug: "physical-activity-and-glycosylphosphatidylinositol-specific-phospholipas-22aede4f",
    titleZh: "不同年龄组和健康状况下体力活动对GPLD1水平的影响",
    titleEn:
      "The Impact of Physical Activity on GPLD1 Levels Across Different Age Groups and Health Conditions",
    summaryZh: "原文信息栏",
    summaryEn: "Why it matters",
    bodyZh:
      "原文信息栏\n\n- Original title: Physical activity and glycosylphosphatidylinositol-specific phospholipase D1 (GPLD1) plasma levels in different age cohorts.\n- Authors: Raza GS, Jokelainen J, Stenbäck V, Sodum N, Karhu T, Gagnon DD et al.\n- Journal/source: PubMed prediabetes lifestyle intervention via PubMed (PloS one)\n- DOI: [10.1371/journal.pone.0349883](https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0349883)\n- PubMed Central: [PMC13225414](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13225414/)\n- PubMed/source link: [https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42224196/](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42224196/)\n- Open-access link: [https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13225414](https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13225414)\n- Evidence used: PubMed abstract + PubMed Central open full-text excerpt + open-access full-text link\n- Published or indexed date: 2026-01-01\n\n为什么值得关注\n\n体力活动被广泛认为对健康有多种益处，包括改善认知功能。它还与 GPLD1 水平的增加有关，GPLD1 是一种可能在认知中发挥作用的蛋白质。然而，年龄、疾病和不同类型的体力活动如何影响 GPLD1 水平尚不清楚。\n\n证据告诉我们什么\n\n研究发现，糖尿病前期受试者的 GPLD1 水平高于老年人和年轻人。患有糖尿病的老年人的 GPLD1 水平明显高于没有糖尿病的人。体力活动没有改变年轻人的 GPLD1 水平，也没有改变糖尿病前期或糖尿病患者的 GPLD1 水平。\n\n应该怎样理解\n\n这项研究提供了 GPLD1 水平如何受到年龄和糖尿病等因素影响的线索，这些因素与认知健康有关。需要注意的是，尽管研究显示了一种关系，但并不能证明因果关系。研究结果也受到特定研究人群限制，可能不适用于所有人。\n\n可以怎么做\n\n了解体力活动、GPLD1 水平和认知健康之间的关系，可以为老年人以及糖尿病前期或糖尿病患者的针对性干预措施提供信息。它也强调了进一步研究 GPLD1、体力活动和认知功能之间机制联系的必要性。\n\n为什么和糖尿病前期有关？\n\n这项研究之所以相关，是因为它探索了体力活动、认知健康和代谢健康之间的交叉点，提供了可以为糖尿病前期和糖尿病等状况的生活方式干预措施提供信息的见解。\n\n你可以带走的重点\n\n- 体力活动对GPLD1水平的影响因年龄和健康状况而异。\n- 糖尿病前期个体表现出更高的GPLD1水平。\n- 老年人中的糖尿病与更高的GPLD1水平相关。\n- 体力活动没有改变糖尿病前期或糖尿病患者的GPLD1。\n- 需要进一步的研究来理解GPLD1在认知中的作用。",
    bodyEn:
      "Why it matters\n\nPhysical activity is known to have numerous health benefits, including improved cognitive function. It is also linked to increased levels of GPLD1, a protein that may play a role in cognition. However, how factors like age, disease, and different types of physical activity affect GPLD1 levels is not well understood.\n\nWhat the evidence says\n\nPrediabetic subjects had higher GPLD1 levels than elderly and young adults. Elderly adults with diabetes had significantly higher GPLD1 levels than those without diabetes. Physical activity did not change GPLD1 levels in young adults, and it did not alter GPLD1 levels in individuals with prediabetes or diabetes.\n\nWhat this means\n\nThe study suggests that GPLD1 levels, which may relate to cognitive health, are influenced by age and diabetes. It is important to note that while the study suggests a relationship, it does not prove causation. The findings are also limited by the specific populations studied and may not apply to everyone.\n\nWhat readers can do\n\nUnderstanding the relationship between physical activity, GPLD1 levels, and cognitive health can inform targeted interventions for older adults and people with prediabetes or diabetes. It also highlights the need for further research into the mechanistic links between GPLD1, physical activity, and cognitive function.\n\nWhy This Matters for Prediabetes\n\nThe study is relevant because it explores the intersection of physical activity, cognitive health, and metabolic health, providing insights that could inform lifestyle interventions for conditions like prediabetes and diabetes.\n\nPractical Takeaways\n\n- Physical activity's impact on GPLD1 levels varies with age and health status.\n- Prediabetic individuals show higher GPLD1 levels.\n- Diabetes in the elderly is associated with higher GPLD1 levels.\n- Physical activity did not alter GPLD1 in those with prediabetes or diabetes.\n- Further research is needed to understand the role of GPLD1 in cognition.",
